<!-- This vignette is precomputed: edit georgiacivicdata.Rmd.orig and run
vignettes/precompile.R. It calls the live API, which CRAN builds can't. -->

georgiacivicdata reads the [Georgia Civic Data](https://georgiacivicdata.org)
API: cleaned public data about Georgia's schools and communities. This
article walks through the everyday workflow: find a dataset, understand it,
get its rows, and cite it.

```{r setup, message = FALSE}
library(georgiacivicdata)
library(dplyr)
```

## Find data

`gcd_search()` ranks datasets from plain words, in English or Spanish:

```{r search}
gcd_search("students missing a lot of school", n = 5) |>
  select(rank, name, title, year_min, year_max)
```

`gcd_datasets()` is the whole catalog, one row per dataset. Every argument
narrows it on the server:

```{r catalog}
catalog <- gcd_datasets()
count(catalog, main_topic, kind)

gcd_datasets(main_topic = "education", level = "districts", year = 2025) |>
  select(name, title)
```

## Understand a dataset

`gcd_describe()` prints what a dataset holds, where it comes from, its
years and detail levels, the columns that identify a row (its *grain*) and
the notes its data contract declares:

```{r describe}
gcd_describe("graduation_rate_4_year_cohort")
```

`gcd_columns()` is the column dictionary. For each metric it gives the
unit and, for counts, whether it is a rate's numerator or denominator:

```{r columns}
gcd_columns("graduation_rate_4_year_cohort") |>
  select(column, type, role, unit, metric_component)
```

Some categorical columns have no total. `gcd_describe()` lists them under
**"Pick one value of"**: for example, `grade_cluster` and `subject` in
`ccrpi_content_mastery`, whose values must not be mixed in one summary.

```{r single}
d <- gcd_describe("ccrpi_content_mastery")
d$filters |>
  filter(kind == "categorical") |>
  select(param, has_total, requires_single_value)
```

## Get rows

`gcd_rows()` returns a tibble typed from the schema. Codes stay character
(leading zeros survive), years are integers, and proportions stay on the 0-1
scale. Every place comes with its name beside its code.

### By name or by code

Name a district, school or county and the package turns it into a code.
Names match exactly, ignoring case, accents and a trailing "County", and
former names work too:

```{r by-name}
riverwood <- gcd_rows(
  "graduation_rate_4_year_cohort",
  district = "Fulton County",
  school = "Riverwood High School",   # a former name
  demographic = "all",
  year = 2021:2025
)
riverwood |>
  select(year, district_code, school_code, school_name, graduation_rate)
```

A name that matches more than one place stops with the candidates instead
of guessing. "Eastside Elementary School" is in several districts, and
Fulton County has an old and a new "Milton High School":

```{r ambiguous, error = TRUE}
gcd_rows("graduation_rate_4_year_cohort", school = "Milton High School",
         district = "Fulton County")
```

Codes work everywhere names do. A school is identified by its district and
school codes together, written `"DISTRICT-SCHOOL"` in `school_id`:

```{r by-code}
gcd_rows("graduation_rate_4_year_cohort", school_id = "660-0106",
         demographic = "all", year = 2025, quiet = TRUE) |>
  select(year, school_code, school_name, num_graduates, num_cohort, graduation_rate)
```

### Years and detail levels

`year = 2021:2025` asks for a range; `year = c(2015, 2025)` asks for those
two years only. Each call returns one **detail level**: schools, districts,
states, counties or tracts, whatever the dataset publishes. The default is
the finest level (counties for Census datasets). Columns that would be
empty at a level are left out:

```{r detail}
state <- gcd_rows("graduation_rate_4_year_cohort", detail = "states",
                  demographic = "all", year = c(2015, 2025), quiet = TRUE)
state
```

To combine levels, call once per level and bind the rows.

### What `NA` means

A missing metric usually means the value was **suppressed** to protect
privacy (small counts), **not zero**. The schema's `null_semantics` says
which, and `gcd_rows()` prints the API's advisory when a result holds
suppressed values:

```{r suppressed}
small <- gcd_rows("graduation_rate_4_year_cohort", district = "Appling County",
                  year = 2024, detail = "districts")
small |>
  select(demographic, num_graduates, num_cohort, graduation_rate)
```

The advisories stay with the result in `gcd_meta(small)$advisories`;
`quiet = TRUE` (or `options(gcd.quiet = TRUE)`) silences the messages.

### Totals beside subgroups

Nothing is pinned by default, so a result holds the `all` total beside every
subgroup, and subgroups from different categories overlap (a student is in
a race group, a gender group and an economic group at once). Never add
them up:

```{r totals}
fulton <- gcd_rows("graduation_rate_4_year_cohort", district = "Fulton County",
                   detail = "districts", year = 2025, quiet = TRUE)

fulton |>
  summarise(
    published_total = num_cohort[demographic == "all"],
    sum_of_every_row = sum(num_cohort, na.rm = TRUE)
  )
```

Use the published `all` row for totals, and filter one category
(`demographic_category = "race"`) to compare groups.

### Which values exist: `gcd_values()`

When a filter returns nothing, `gcd_values()` shows which combinations
exist under the other filters, with row counts, without downloading rows:

```{r values}
gcd_values("ccrpi_content_mastery", c("grade_cluster", "subject"),
           year = 2025, detail = "districts")
```

## Lookup tables

The six lookup tables (`districts`, `schools`, `counties`, `tracts`,
`block_groups`, `demographics`) hold more than names: school addresses,
coordinates, grade spans, locale and charter status, tract populations.
`gcd_dimension()` returns them:

```{r schools}
schools <- gcd_dimension("schools",
                         columns = c("district_code", "school_code", "locale"))
schools
```

**School codes repeat across districts**, so always join on both codes:

```{r join}
schools |> count(school_code, sort = TRUE) |> head(3)

high_schools <- gcd_rows("graduation_rate_4_year_cohort", detail = "schools",
                         demographic = "all", year = 2025, quiet = TRUE) |>
  left_join(schools, by = c("district_code", "school_code"))

high_schools |>
  filter(!is.na(locale)) |>
  group_by(locale) |>
  summarise(schools = n(),
            graduation_rate = sum(num_graduates, na.rm = TRUE) /
              sum(num_cohort[!is.na(num_graduates)], na.rm = TRUE))
```

That last step pools the rate from its counts. Averaging rates
(`mean(graduation_rate)`) would weigh a school of 40 like a school of 2,000.

## Cite the data

The data is CC BY 4.0. `gcd_citation()` writes the credit, from a result
or a dataset name:

```{r cite}
gcd_citation(riverwood)
```

## Caching

Metadata (the catalog, schemas and lookup tables) is cached for the R
session. Rows can also be cached on disk, if you ask:

```{r cache, eval = FALSE}
options(gcd.cache = TRUE)   # or cache = TRUE in gcd_rows()
```

Cached responses are kept with their `ETag` in
`tools::R_user_dir("georgiacivicdata", "cache")`, and each repeat asks the
server whether the data changed: an unchanged answer reuses the saved copy
without downloading, and a new data release downloads afresh (a response the
server marks fresh, for five minutes, is reused without asking).
`gcd_cache_clear()` empties both caches.
